**(i) Trade blocs**

A **trade bloc** (regional trade bloc or economic bloc) is a group of countries, usually in the same region, which agree to **reduce or remove the barriers to trade** (tariffs, quotas and other restrictions) among themselves, in order to increase their trade and promote the economic growth of the members. They differ in the degree of integration:

- a **free trade area** (the members remove the tariffs among themselves but each keeps its own external tariffs, as in the former NAFTA, now the USMCA, and the African Continental Free Trade Area, AfCFTA, launched in 2018 and in operation since 2021);
- a **customs union** (a free trade area with a **common external tariff**, for example the CEMAC of which Cameroon is a member, and MERCOSUR);
- a **common market** (a customs union plus the free movement of labour and capital);
- an **economic and monetary union** (a common market with a common currency and common economic policies, for example the European Union and the Eurozone; the CEMAC also has a common currency, the CFA franc).

Other examples: ECOWAS in West Africa, SADC, ASEAN. Trade blocs aim to enlarge the market, to increase competition, to attract investment and to give the members greater bargaining strength in the world trade, but they can divert trade from non-members and may hurt the weaker members.

**(ii) Sustainable development**

**Sustainable development** is the development that **meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of the future generations to meet their own needs** (Brundtland Report, "Our Common Future", 1987). It aims at a balance between three pillars: **economic growth** (a decent standard of living and jobs), **social equity** (the satisfaction of the basic needs of all, the reduction of poverty and the fair sharing of the benefits), and **environmental protection** (the conservation of resources and ecosystems, and the limit of pollution). It was set as a global goal at the Rio Earth Summit (1992, with Agenda 21), and is the basis of the 17 **Sustainable Development Goals** adopted by the United Nations in 2015 for 2030. It implies using renewable resources at a rate no faster than they regenerate, and using non-renewable resources carefully, and passing on to the next generation an environment in a good state.
